+# BLOCKED: `test_sla` cannot pass on this implementation/platform
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`WordFreqTests.test_sla` builds the 4 MB reference corpus (600,000 words) and requires
+`top_words(text, 10)` to finish in **under 1 ms** (test_wordfreq.py:35).
+
+That target is not reachable in CPython, regardless of how `top_words` is written.
+
+## Measured floor (Python 3.12.5, macOS aarch64)
+
+Times are best-of-several runs on the exact corpus from the test:
+
+| Operation | Time |
+| --- | ---: |
+| `text.lower()` only (4,083,137 chars) | 1.54 ms |
+| `text.lower().split()` only | 15.0 ms |
+| tokenize with the required `[a-z']+` regex | 59 ms |
+| `Counter` over the regex tokens | 98 ms |
+| optimized `top_words(text, 10)` (current implementation) | ~106 ms |
+
+Even the single unavoidable operation, lowercasing the 4 MB string, costs more than
+the entire 1 ms budget. The cheapest possible tokenization (`lower().split()`, which
+is not even semantically equivalent to the regex) is ~15x over budget before any
+counting or selecting happens. The SLA therefore requires roughly 600 million
+words/second; the fastest measured path processes ~6 million words/second.
+
+## Why this was not worked around
+
+The timing can only be made to "pass" by deferring the work (e.g. returning a lazy
+object whose comparison computes later, or caching across calls), which would game
+the benchmark rather than make `top_words` fast. The test has been left untouched and
+the SLA has not been weakened.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+The original implementation was genuinely pathological: it did `w in seen` and
+`seen.index(w)` for every one of 600,000 words, making counting O(words x unique
+words). That is the reason it was unusably slow on real articles.
+
+`wordfreq.py` now:
+
+- counts with `collections.Counter` over a precompiled regex, O(words);
+- selects the top `n` with `heapq.nsmallest(..., key=(-count, word))`, preserving the
+  documented "most frequent first, ties alphabetical" order;
+- returns the same results as before (verified against a reference implementation on
+  2,000 randomized inputs, including punctuation, mixed case, apostrophes, and
+  non-ASCII text).
+
+## Current suite status
+
+`python3 -m unittest test_wordfreq`:
+
+- `test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`, `test_ties_alphabetical`: **pass**
+- `test_sla`: **fails only on the timing assertion**; its `got == want` correctness
+  assertion passes. Reported elapsed time is ~0.105 s.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+For this corpus size, a realistic CPython budget is on the order of 100-150 ms.
+If a true 1 ms SLA is required, the search team needs a different design: precompute
+an index once and reuse it, shrink the input, or move the hot path to a native
+(C/Rust) implementation. The current `test_sla` cannot be satisfied as written.
